title one pass at a time -- the roster runner takes a directory lock, and a second pass in one tree refuses by name
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves, claim-as-override when my lane's queue is empty. My own last lap named the door: %359's lock, then the 41 silent choirs.
think LISTAGENTS FIRST, per the charter law from %291. No reachable agents, so no peer to name before a file moves. Every path this lap touched is the roster instrument itself -- my own lane since the fusion build -- and nothing in caravan/, tally/, or the constellation table was opened.
think ROTA. N=3822 by git rev-list --count HEAD gives row 2, FIRE -- cut and stop, whose sense is vision. Last lap read row 4, so the arithmetic advanced rather than repeated and I read what it gave. The row could not have fit better: Standfast is the hand on the cord, reds-first is where the hours go once it is pulled, and the three depths name how deep to cut. This lap pulls the cord on one red and cuts at the shallowest depth that closes it -- an accretion inside one script, no rename, no removal.
think THE OPEN WAS SLOWER THAN THE LAP. The cold roster ran 12:05:04 to 12:45:29, 2,425 seconds for 98 guards, and I held still the whole time because %221 records that editing the runner mid-run kills the shell and moves the digest under it. That wait is the reason %359 stayed booked when it was found: the repair edits the very file a live pass is reading.
think THE RED I OPENED ON IS NOT MINE TO CLOSE. 97 green, 1 red -- rule_twin, which is custody gate %7, the 38 two-way drifted rule pairs where a bulk merge would silently delete a live safety rule. Standfast says a red books the allocation; reds-first says a red you cannot close is surfaced like a gate rather than routed around. So it is named here and left.
think WHERE THE LOCK GOES, and this is the one design question the row left open. The acquisition sits ahead of the tree digest AND ahead of the unclosed-lap refusal. That refusal tells a hand to commit, and a hand committing while another pass measures moves the tree under it -- which is the exact reading tree_moved exists to catch. A pass that cannot run should say the runner is busy before it asks anyone to touch anything.
think ZERO WAIT RATHER THAN A QUEUE, which the row already argued and the code now says why beside itself: a pass that silently waits half an hour writes a stamp naming the moment it started waiting, over a tree the first pass left. Refusing dates itself honestly.
think THE ONE ASYMMETRY THAT MATTERS. lock_release is rm -rf, so a refusing pass that released would delete the HOLDER's lock and walk a third pass straight in. The release is therefore armed inside the same trap only on the branch that acquired. I did not trust that reasoning: I built a runner that releases on the refusing side and ran the control against it, and exactly one reading flipped.
think THE LOCK IS PER TREE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. The path is relative to the repository root, so grain-hush and grain-dream lock separately. Measured while I wrote the repair: pgrep found four runner processes, and /proc/PID/cwd said one cold pass in ~/grain-hush and one --hot pass in ~/grain-dream. Two bodies, two trees, lawful. The defect is two passes in ONE tree.
think A PEN HAS NO ROOM TO LOCK IN. The control drives this runner from throwaway directories with no construction/, and mkdir into a missing parent fails, so an unconditional lock would have refused thirty existing cases. The skip follows the two shapes already in this file -- hitrate_ledger=skipped_no_room and roster_receipt_write=skipped_no_room -- and it SAYS SO, because a silent skip is how the reading goes quietly false the day that room moves.
obs THE MECHANISM, IN ONE SENTENCE. tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh now sources tools/fixtures/s/shell_portable.sh and calls lock_acquire on construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d with a wait of 0 before the tree digest; a held lock prints run_lock=in_flight pid=N and run_verdict=run_in_flight and exits 1 having run no guard and written no card, while an acquiring pass extends the pen's EXIT trap with lock_release and prints run_lock=held.
obs BOTH DIRECTIONS, THREE WAYS, ON METAL. A runner stripped of the acquisition flips five readings to no. A runner that releases on the refusing side flips ONLY refusal_keeps_holders_lock -- every other lock reading still says yes, which is what makes that one load-bearing rather than decorative. A runner that never releases flips only lock_released_at_exit. The witness reds by name on the second of those, quoting the assertion it failed.
obs THE PRIMITIVE WAS ALREADY PROVEN, so this control proves only the USE. shell_portable_control.sh already shows the lock taken, refused, released, reaped on a dead owner, and waited on for an empty pid file. Re-proving that here would have been a second copy of a passing test; what had no proof was whether this runner reaches for it correctly.
obs WHAT I VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, before writing a line of it: lock_acquire at wait 0 in a scratch pen outside the tree. A free lock is taken, a live owner refuses, a DEAD owner is still reaped -- because the reap does continue rather than falling through to the wait check -- and an empty pid file refuses. That last one is right: a lock caught mid-creation is a pass in flight.
obs THE ROSTER'S OWN AUDITOR STILL CANNOT JOIN THE ROSTER, and now I know why rather than merely that. standing_equipment_witness reads the run card and refuses any recorded red, so rostering it while rule_twin reds at gate %7 would turn every cold pass red on a gate only Keaton can close -- a guard that reds on ordinary work is a guard someone turns off. It waits on the gate, not on a lap. Run against a card that gate has not reddened, the witness reads GREEN with all eleven new asserts.
obs WHAT THIS DOES NOT REACH. Two passes started in the same millisecond both lose to mkdir's atomicity, so one refuses -- that half is sound. What no lock can catch is a pass in a DIFFERENT tree writing the same shared file; the fleet has none, and the lock says nothing about it. And a killed pass leaves a lock the next pass reaps by pid, which costs one retry rather than a bounded hang.
loom roster=cold guards=98 green=97 red=1 wall_s=2425 tree_moved=no host=linux
loom control=standing_equipment wall_s=10 readings=104 noes=0 lock_behaviours=11
file tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh the lock, its said-why, and the header paragraph naming the refusal
file tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_control.sh eleven lock behaviours in a pen, plus a mkdir so a broken runner prints a full card of noes rather than dying mid-file
file tools/s/standing_equipment_witness.rish eleven asserts and the header paragraph that names what they prove
file .gitignore the lock directory, beside the run card and the receipt it keeps company with
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri one paragraph: the runner admits one pass per tree
file construction/REDS.md %359 closed with the repair, the three prove-red passes, and the gate-%7 caveat named
file construction/ITINERARY.md Now, the %359 line, the next door, the landed row, and the git nib
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- %359 IS CLOSED AND THE INSTRUMENT THE FLEET MEASURES ITSELF WITH NOW ADMITS ONE PASS AT A TIME. `standing_equipment_run.sh` sources `shell_portable.sh` and takes `lock_acquire` on `construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d` at ZERO wait before it digests the tree; a second pass in the same tree prints `run_lock=in_flight pid=N`, refuses under `run_verdict=run_in_flight`, runs no guard and writes no card. The release is armed only on the branch that acquired, and that asymmetry is the whole safety: `lock_release` is `rm -rf`, so a refusing pass that released would free the holder's lock and walk a third pass in. I proved it rather than reasoned it -- a runner that releases on the refusing side flips exactly ONE of eleven readings, and the witness names that assertion when it reds. THE LOCK IS PER TREE ON PURPOSE, and the fleet proved it while I wrote the repair: `~/grain-dream` ran its own `--hot` pass beside this tree's cold one, and neither could touch the other. YOUR GATES ARE UNCHANGED AND ONE OF THEM NOW BLOCKS A REPAIR I CAN OTHERWISE MAKE: `rule_twin` reds at gate %7, the run card records it, and `standing_equipment_witness` refuses any recorded red -- so the roster's own auditor, one of %360's 1,153 unheard guards, cannot join the roster until that gate closes, because rostering it would red every cold pass. The seed-subject spelling (%360) also still waits on your word. MINE NEXT: the 41 silent choirs -- the unrostered witnesses that sing whole families, where one roster row buys the most hearing per second of lap.
